enhancement.detailedExplanation:
Section 105 BNSS represents one of the most transformative procedural reforms in the 2023 Sanhita, fundamentally addressing decades of judicial criticism regarding staged police recoveries and hostile panchas.

Under the repealed Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973 (Section 100), searches were conducted in the presence of two independent local witnesses who signed a paper panchnama. In practice, independent witnesses frequently turned hostile during trial years later, claiming they signed blank papers at the police station. Conversely, corrupt officers could plant contraband or weapons and create fictitious seizure memos.

Section 105 dismantles this vulnerability by mandating **audio-video electronic recording** (specifically noting smartphones as an accessible tool). The police officer executing a search or seizure must video-record:
1. The entry into the premises;
2. The discovery of the concealed articles;
3. The physical packaging and sealing of the seized items;
4. The contemporaneous drafting of the inventory list; and
5. The witnesses actually appending their signatures to the seizure list.

Furthermore, to prevent post-hoc video editing or digital tampering, the officer is statutorily mandated to forward the unedited recording without delay to the competent Magistrate. When tendered in evidence at trial, the digital video must be authenticated by a Certificate under Section 63(4) of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023.
